welcome back to rmix logic data science  in this session we will introduce  biomedical data analysis techniques and  approaches that you can master and adopt  to help you conduct better research  the goal of this program is to introduce  the need for data science and  application to biomedical projects many  of such projects require a good  understanding of various omics data  types steps to process raw data and  structured for analysis as well as know  how to extract meaningful information  from such data sets to visualization  analysis and machine learning  importantly here you will learn to link  biological phenomena with data patterns  for meaningful interpretation  before we go on let's review the program  plan and objectives  the syllabus of the program is organized  into three different parts introduction  methods  and applications  in the introduction you will be prepared  to deal with relevant terminology and  key concepts to refresh your memory and  get you started  the methods part will give you a  practical sense of commonly used methods  and functions for analysis of mixed data  starting from exploratory to statistical  testing supervised and unsupervised  machine learning as well as deep  learning  finally the project section will offer a  sense of application for how these  methods are applied to real data sets  completing the full program will give  you the confidence that you understand  the analysis and can apply it  independently to a data set of your  choice  each session is designed to cover the  topics in a practical and informative  way  oftentimes these session videos are  going to be sent to you by email  in each session you will also find  additional links to asynchronous  resources that review and enrich the  cover topic  depending on how far you want to go with  the program you can choose to stay  within the scope of the syllabus or  explore beyond what is covered in each  session with asynchronous materials that  are included  in this session i will try to cover  everything you need to know to make the  most of your time and benefit from this  program  in the future we will also schedule a q  a session where you will be able to  submit your questions and get answers in  an interactive session before you jump  into the data science curriculum we do  recommend that you complete the  introductory courses which you also have  access to including the introduction to  bioinformatics bytes and molecules as  well as a basic overview of rn python  coding for bioinformatics analysis if  you're already familiar with these  topics let's discuss what specifically  will be covered in each part of this  program if a lot of what we are talking  about when we say words like  transcriptomics genomics metagenomics  does not really make sense yet if you're  not sure what omix data looks like and  maybe you have no clue what  dimensionality reduction really means  the first part of the program will make  sure that you're familiar with these  topics and understand the terminology we  will try to answer questions about  processing or preparing data for  analysis we will walk through the  concepts behind finding signal in noisy  data and explain why it is important to  understand how the molecular  characteristics of data objects can be  associated with a concept that we can  interpret  here you will find three main options  there's going to be a logical approach  which will be based on analysis that  will explain most concepts using excel  and the user-friendly t-bioinfo platform  you will have another track which is  called bioml in r for those who are more  familiar and comfortable with r and r  studio and finally you will see the  bioml in python which focuses on the  python programming language each one of  these courses covers similar topics  however the level of complexity and  challenges with syntax will be very  different we recommend that you take the  biomelogic course first and then decide  to finish either the python or the r  coding courses separately because the  syntax is quite different from each  other and it can become confusing if you  start working on both at the same time  coding exercises and syntax in each one  of the coding lessons will rely on  packages that have to be installed on  your computer to run  however we will also provide you with an  online environment where you can read  and understand the syntax run the code  on example datasets and gain access to  jupyter notebooks to develop code of  your own and submit assignments  ultimately you might choose to copy all  of the code and save on your own  personal machine  to make sure that everything is working  make sure that you're not assuming that  code will work exactly the same way  there might be many different technical  challenges for example you might need to  install python or r  maybe you will need to set up your  coding environment which includes  setting the working directory loading  packages and make sure that you overcome  the technical limitations which often  come up and are specific to your own  computer so the safest way is to utilize  the online environment that we provide  and after you are ready to transition  and you understand how the code works to  try and set up everything on your  computer  after completing this introduction we  will jump into machine learning topics  unsupervised and supervised learning as  well as feature selection these methods  are often used for various tasks such as  visualization organization of data  finding signal in noisy omics data as  well as things like predictive analysis  and feature selection to be able to use  these methods you need to understand how  they work what are their inputs  variables settings and available  packages that combine multiple functions  to make the methods easy to deploy  you will learn how to load data select a  method and run it to get an output of  that particular model and try to  understand what it found  then we will start our review of  additional analytical methods that are  useful for biomedical discovery  including network analysis such as  pathway analysis generalized linear  models that are used to distinguish  between expression profiles of  transcripts as well as deep learning  models that can be used for specific  types of situations to improve accuracy  and decrease feature engineering that a  lot of conventional machine learning  relies on we will also talk about the  need to combine various machine learning  models into a series of steps to achieve  more sophisticated tasks and finally we  will talk about different use cases that  can show how these methods that we  review have been used by researchers to  find meaningful insights in complex  experiments biological samples animal  models or even patient data  with the few examples that we review as  case studies you can then develop a  project of your own or follow  asynchronous tutorials to reproduce an  analysis that you can improve upon  turning it into a novel and personal  study on the omnilogic portal you will  find a more detailed review of each data  set that we use in our exercises with  additional tutorials method  clarifications and links to research  articles where the authors demonstrate  the utility of machine learning methods  for their own findings now let's take a  brief moment to explain what kind of  resources you will have access to during  the program and how exactly to use them  one of the major challenges in omics  data analysis is related to accurate  reproducible and effective processing of  large-scale data sets that represent  gigabytes or even terabytes of data such  large quantities prevents many people to  gain practical hands-on feel for these  pipelines which are critical to  understand where the data you analyze  comes from  to be able to do that we have prepared a  number of example projects that are  essentially demos these pipelines do not  have to run they don't use computational  resources and they demonstrate the  inputs and outputs as well as the logic  of analysis for each example you will  also find that a lot of these pipelines  are color coded and provide a very clear  explanation of each step with conditions  methods and settings that you have to  know for reproducible workflows you will  also get to practice all of this code in  the online console where you will find  predefined answers and you can try to  run the code simply by modifying it  according to what was explained in the  syntax section  you will find a lot of these clear  explanations in our courses for example  this course on introduction to data  science in python  and introduction to data science and r  you will find that many of the same  methods are covered but with little  variation in the way the data is  prepared  analyzed and the outputs that these  methods produce
 

